I've uploaded a Python repository at /workspace/{{ workspace_dir_name }}.
Your goal: locate the following {{ num_targets }} targets (docstrings removed) and write a clear, accurate docstring for each:

{{ target_list }}

Workflow (strict; follow exactly):

1) OVERVIEW (MANDATORY BROAD SEARCH FIRST)
   - BEFORE opening any specific file, run 1–2 broad searches that scan the ENTIRE repository root /workspace/{{ workspace_dir_name }} for the most distinctive keywords from each target's description.
   - Acceptable commands (examples you should use exactly or adapt):
     - rg -n --hidden --glob '!venv' "keyword1|keyword2" /workspace/{{ workspace_dir_name }}
     - grep -RInE "keyword1|keyword2" /workspace/{{ workspace_dir_name }}
     - find /workspace/{{ workspace_dir_name }} -name "*.py" -print
   - These broad searches must cover the whole repo tree. Do NOT open any full file contents before these broad searches complete.

2) MULTI-ROUND REFINEMENT (AT LEAST 2 DISTINCT SEARCH ROUNDS)
   - After reviewing broad search results, run at least TWO different, narrower searches that refine candidates based on signals you saw.
   - Refinement examples:
     - Restrict to a subdirectory: rg -n "keyword" /workspace/{{ workspace_dir_name }}/subpkg
     - Search for definition lines or identifiers: rg -n "^def .*target_name|^class .*TargetName" /workspace/{{ workspace_dir_name }}
     - Search for surrounding terms seen in matches: rg -n "helper_function|specific_constant" /workspace/{{ workspace_dir_name }}
   - Each refinement must be motivated by the previous results (state briefly what you learned and why you ran the next search).

3) CANDIDATE MANAGEMENT (REPORT BEFORE READING)
   - After refinement, produce a short candidate list (paths + brief note why each is plausible). The candidate list must be <= 10 before any file reads.
   - Only when candidates are narrowed to <= 3 should you open full file contents for deep reading.

4) READ AFTER NARROWING (STRICT LIMITS)
   - Do NOT open full files early. Allowed lightweight inspection before narrowing:
     - Show filename matches, surrounding match lines, or small context snippets (rg already provides line-level context).
     - Use head/tail or sed to view ≤30 lines if necessary for quick disambiguation.
   - Full file reads (file_editor view with no view_range OR range >100 lines) are permitted ONLY after you have ≤ 3 plausible candidates and have listed them explicitly.
   - When you do read files fully, state the candidate count and which files you are opening.

5) VERIFY & WRITE DOCSTRINGS
   - Confirm the located definition matches the provided description by comparing behavior, parameters, return values, and side effects.
   - Insert a triple-quoted Python docstring in the definition covering:
     - One-line summary (50–80 chars).
     - Explanation of parameters, types, defaults, and behavior.
     - Return value(s) and types.
     - Important side effects, exceptions raised, and thread-safety/IO notes if relevant.
   - Do not modify code outside the docstring unless absolutely necessary; if you must change code, state why.

6) LOGGING & EXPLANATIONS (REQUIRED)
   - For each search step you run, log the exact command you ran and a 1–2 line rationale for it.
   - After refinement rounds, list the candidate files with short justifications.
   - Before any full-file read, confirm: "Opening full file(s): <paths>" and explain why they are ≤3 and likely correct.

Forbidden behaviors (will be judged non-compliant):
- Opening a specific file (full read) before running repo-wide broad searches.
- Reading full files early (before narrowing to ≤3).
- Committing to a single candidate after only one search round.

Be thorough and explicit in your search log and reasoning as you proceed. Follow the broad-then-narrow, multi-round refinement, and read-after-narrowing rules exactly.
